  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nUpper air analysis early this afternoon shows two separate \ntroughs that have phased into one over the central Plains \nstates. At the surface, a 1002 mb low is located over northwest \nMissouri with a cold front stretched from north-central Missouri\ninto the southern Plains states. Out ahead of the front, strong\nsoutherly winds are in place with peak gusts generally ranging \nfrom 30 to 40 mph. Winds are expected to ease some through this \nevening as the surface low approaches, but will remain elevated \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nScattered showers are ongoing over west-central Illinois and are \nforecast to overspread the remainder of the area through this \nevening. In addition, there is a low chance for a few isolated \nthunderstorms with the focus being confined to areas west of a \nBloomington to Effingham line. Although instability is forecast to \nbe weak (MLCAPE <300 J/kg), strong wind shear will be in place with \n0-1 km SRH approaching 250-300 m2/s2. This paired with strong \nconvergence along the approaching cold front and large-scale ascent \nfrom the upper wave may support a few clusters of marginally severe \nconvection through this evening. Primary threats with any stronger \nstorms will be damaging wind gusts and perhaps a couple tornadoes. \nDespite climatologically high PWATs (1.50\"+), rain amounts look\nlargely be around 0.50\" or less. The latest suite of 12Z \nguidance shows a few heavier swaths of close to 1 inch falling \nover central parts of the state, likely tied to any areas of \nheavier showers or convection.\n\nCooler and breezy conditions filter in behind the cold front for \nWednesday and Thursday. Some wrap around precipitation on the \nbackside of the departing low may promote isolated to scattered \nlight showers later Thursday into Friday. Surface high pressure \nsettles into the area by the weekend, turning things mostly dry and \nseasonal through early next week.",
